;; OPC UA, Modbus TCP and CAN on a dev board, for bench testing.
;;
;; The device itself: silt-sim reads /etc/silt-sim.json, validates it, and
;; publishes a tag table into /dev/shm. Every protocol daemon serves that
;; table, so one file describes the device and no two protocols can
;; disagree about which tag lives where. The default config is a pump:
;; temperature, pressure, rpm, run hours, a fault bit, and a writable
;; setpoint and run flag. Editing that file changes the device; nothing
;; about it is fixed in the image.
;;
;; OPC UA: silt-opcua serves the same table on port 4840, anonymous. Each
;; tag with an opcua name becomes a variable under Objects, as
;; ns=1;s=<device>.<node>, so the default pump reads at
;; ns=1;s=pump-1.Temperature. open62541 is a library and installs no
;; server, which is why the pack carries one; it is selected by the
;; package, therefore not stated here.
;;
;; Modbus TCP: silt-modbus serves the table on port 502, at the addresses
;; the config gives. A client writing a writable tag writes it into the
;; table, where the other protocols see it. Like open62541, libmodbus is a
;; library, selected by the package; feature:modbus, composed beside this
;; one, states it for any image.
;;
;; CAN: silt-can packs the tags that name a can id into frames and sends
;; them on vcan0 - the default pump puts temperature, pressure and rpm in
;; 0x100 and the fault bit in 0x101 - and writes writable tags back from
;; frames it receives. can-utils (candump, cansend, cangen) is there to
;; watch and drive that bus, and S45vcan creates it at boot, so all of it
;; works on a board with no CAN hardware.
;;
;; Config over HTTP: silt-http accepts a device config on port 8080, checks
;; it with the same validator, saves it and asks the sim core to reload, so
;; a buyer changes the device with curl rather than by editing a file over
;; ssh. GET /tags shows every live value as JSON, which is the quickest way
;; to see what the device is doing without a protocol client at all.
;;
;; MQTT: silt-mqtt publishes each tag to the local broker as it changes, on
;; silt/<device>/<tag>, retained, and accepts writes on
;; silt/<device>/<tag>/set. Northbound, the direction a gateway runs: the
;; field protocols read the device, MQTT carries it to whatever is
;; subscribed. Nothing here talks to the other daemons over MQTT; they
;; share the table.
;;
;; No authentication and no TLS on either. The board already serves
;; anonymous OPC UA, unauthenticated Modbus writes and an open CAN bus, so
;; a password on one endpoint would suggest a protection the device does
;; not have. Lab networks only, and the docs should say so.

(fragment feature:fieldbus-test

  (doc "One simulated device on Modbus, OPC UA, CAN and MQTT, configured over HTTP")

  (requires
    (capability mmu))

  (buildroot
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_SILT_SIM)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_SILT_MODBUS)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_SILT_OPCUA)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_SILT_CAN)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_SILT_HTTP)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_SILT_MQTT)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_CAN_UTILS)

    ;; BusyBox's ip cannot create a link of type vcan or configure a CAN
    ;; interface's bitrate; iproute2's can.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_IPROUTE2)

    ;; The Pi kernels are built with CONFIG_MODULE_COMPRESS_XZ, and
    ;; Buildroot's BusyBox has FEATURE_SEAMLESS_XZ off, so BusyBox modprobe
    ;; cannot load vcan.ko.xz. kmod's modprobe can, given xz.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_KMOD)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_KMOD_TOOLS)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_XZ)

    ;; /etc/init.d/S45vcan: creates vcan0 at boot under BusyBox init.
    (path-append BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY "overlay"))

  (linux
    ;; What vcan0 needs. Modules, not built in: bcmrpi3_defconfig already
    ;; says m for all three, and a board with real CAN hardware loads its
    ;; own controller driver beside them.
    (m CONFIG_CAN)
    (m CONFIG_CAN_RAW)
    (m CONFIG_CAN_VCAN)))
